1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:05,470
The phone in your pocket contains gold,
copper and dozens of other metals,

2
00:00:05,510 --> 00:00:09,170
and every gram of them
was dug out of the ground.

3
00:00:09,210 --> 00:00:15,133
In South Africa, the deepest gold mines
reach about four kilometres underground.

4
00:00:15,173 --> 00:00:20,114
And from one tonne of gold ore,
you get only about five grams of gold.

5
00:00:20,154 --> 00:00:23,511
So how do we find these metals,
get them out of the rock

6
00:00:23,551 --> 00:00:25,984
— and what does it cost the planet?

7
00:00:26,024 --> 00:00:27,474
Let's dig in.

8
00:00:32,483 --> 00:00:34,683
Most metals aren't found pure.

9
00:00:34,723 --> 00:00:40,729
In rock, they're chemically joined to
other elements, usually oxygen or sulphur.

10
00:00:40,769 --> 00:00:44,481
A rock that contains enough
of a useful mineral to be

11
00:00:44,521 --> 00:00:48,866
worth the cost of mining and
processing is called an ore.

12
00:00:48,906 --> 00:00:50,887
The key words are 'worth the cost':

13
00:00:50,927 --> 00:00:54,356
if there's too little of the
mineral, it isn't an ore.

14
00:00:54,396 --> 00:00:57,342
Refining is the set of
processes that separate

15
00:00:57,382 --> 00:01:00,783
the pure metal from
everything else in the ore.

16
00:01:01,583 --> 00:01:06,203
South Africa is one of the richest
mining countries in the world.

17
00:01:06,243 --> 00:01:09,011
Gold comes from the Witwatersrand basin,

18
00:01:09,051 --> 00:01:12,590
across Gauteng,
the Free State and North West.

19
00:01:12,630 --> 00:01:15,683
Platinum and chrome come from the Bushveld

20
00:01:15,723 --> 00:01:19,127
Igneous Complex in Limpopo and North West.

21
00:01:19,167 --> 00:01:22,483
Iron ore is mined at Sishen,
and manganese in

22
00:01:22,523 --> 00:01:26,263
the Kalahari field,
both in the Northern Cape.

23
00:01:26,303 --> 00:01:30,856
Coal comes mainly from Mpumalanga
and Limpopo, diamonds from

24
00:01:30,896 --> 00:01:35,723
Kimberley and coastal deposits,
and copper from Phalaborwa.

25
00:01:35,763 --> 00:01:40,934
Mining brings jobs, foreign income
and raw materials for industry.

26
00:01:41,850 --> 00:01:47,852
These minerals end up all around you: gold
in jewellery, electronics and medicine;

27
00:01:47,892 --> 00:01:50,283
platinum in catalytic converters,

28
00:01:50,323 --> 00:01:54,041
which clean car exhaust;
chrome in stainless steel,

29
00:01:54,081 --> 00:01:56,767
and manganese to make steel stronger;

30
00:01:56,807 --> 00:02:00,377
iron in the steel itself;
coal in power stations;

31
00:02:00,417 --> 00:02:06,072
diamonds in cutting and drilling tools;
and copper in electrical wiring.

32
00:02:06,983 --> 00:02:11,197
How a mineral is mined depends
on how deep the ore lies.

33
00:02:11,237 --> 00:02:16,622
For deep ore, like gold and platinum,
we use shaft mining: a vertical shaft

34
00:02:16,662 --> 00:02:22,107
is sunk into the ground, and horizontal
tunnels are dug out to the ore.

35
00:02:22,147 --> 00:02:27,034
Miners, machines and ore travel
up and down in cages and lifts.

36
00:02:27,074 --> 00:02:30,431
For ore close to the surface,
like iron, coal,

37
00:02:30,471 --> 00:02:34,104
copper and diamonds,
we use open cast mining.

38
00:02:34,144 --> 00:02:38,782
The soil and rock on top, called
the overburden, is stripped away,

39
00:02:38,822 --> 00:02:44,236
and the ore is dug from a huge pit
that deepens in steps called benches.

40
00:02:44,276 --> 00:02:49,672
And for small deposits of gold and
diamonds in riverbeds, there's panning:

41
00:02:49,712 --> 00:02:54,079
gravel is swirled with water
in a pan, heavy particles sink,

42
00:02:54,119 --> 00:02:57,554
and the lighter sand washes over the edge.

43
00:02:58,450 --> 00:03:01,448
Once the ore is out, refining begins.

44
00:03:01,488 --> 00:03:07,537
Stage one is physical separation, which
doesn't change the substances chemically.

45
00:03:07,577 --> 00:03:12,537
The rock is crushed and ground
into small pieces or a fine powder.

46
00:03:12,577 --> 00:03:16,599
Useless rock is sorted out
by hand or sifted away.

47
00:03:16,639 --> 00:03:20,485
Then washing, sieving and gravity
— panning or sluicing

48
00:03:20,525 --> 00:03:24,691
— separate heavy mineral
particles from lighter waste.

49
00:03:25,483 --> 00:03:27,473
Stage two is chemical.

50
00:03:27,513 --> 00:03:32,965
Many metals occur as metal oxides,
so we need to remove the oxygen.

51
00:03:33,005 --> 00:03:39,992
Removing oxygen from a compound is called
reduction; gaining oxygen is oxidation.

52
00:03:40,032 --> 00:03:45,099
The most common reducing agent
is carbon, as coke or charcoal.

53
00:03:45,139 --> 00:03:51,068
At high temperatures, carbon attracts the
oxygen more strongly than the metal does,

54
00:03:51,108 --> 00:03:55,661
so it takes the oxygen and
leaves pure molten metal behind.

55
00:03:55,701 --> 00:04:00,768
Take lead: lead oxide plus carbon
gives lead plus carbon monoxide.

56
00:04:00,808 --> 00:04:04,617
The lead oxide loses oxygen,
so it's reduced.

57
00:04:04,657 --> 00:04:08,175
The carbon gains oxygen, so it's oxidised.

58
00:04:09,083 --> 00:04:11,623
Iron is made in a blast furnace.

59
00:04:11,663 --> 00:04:14,882
Iron ore, coke and
limestone go in at the top,

60
00:04:14,922 --> 00:04:17,923
and hot air is blown in at the bottom.

61
00:04:17,963 --> 00:04:21,257
The carbon burns to form carbon monoxide,

62
00:04:21,297 --> 00:04:25,185
which reduces the iron
oxide to molten iron.

63
00:04:25,225 --> 00:04:28,542
The limestone removes
sandy impurities as slag,

64
00:04:28,582 --> 00:04:32,534
which floats on the molten
iron and is drained off.

65
00:04:32,574 --> 00:04:37,063
For lead, the ore galena —
lead sulphide — is crushed and

66
00:04:37,103 --> 00:04:42,100
roasted in air, forming lead
oxide and sulphur dioxide gas.

67
00:04:42,140 --> 00:04:45,480
The lead oxide is then reduced with coke.

68
00:04:46,383 --> 00:04:49,674
Copper ore is crushed
and heated with oxygen,

69
00:04:49,714 --> 00:04:52,837
giving copper sulphide and iron oxide.

70
00:04:52,877 --> 00:04:55,926
Silica is added, which combines with the

71
00:04:55,966 --> 00:04:59,673
iron oxide to form slag,
and that's removed.

72
00:04:59,713 --> 00:05:05,410
Heating the copper sulphide in air gives
impure copper, which is then purified.

73
00:05:05,450 --> 00:05:07,150
Gold is different.

74
00:05:07,190 --> 00:05:11,545
The ore is crushed, washed and
filtered, then a cyanide solution

75
00:05:11,585 --> 00:05:16,289
dissolves the gold, and zinc
brings it back out of the solution.

76
00:05:16,329 --> 00:05:19,867
With only about five grams
per tonne, producing one

77
00:05:19,907 --> 00:05:24,466
kilogram of gold means processing
two hundred tonnes of ore.

78
00:05:25,550 --> 00:05:27,963
Mining comes at a cost, and because the

79
00:05:28,003 --> 00:05:31,270
Earth's spheres are linked,
the damage spreads.

80
00:05:31,310 --> 00:05:34,981
Mines use huge amounts
of water and electricity.

81
00:05:35,021 --> 00:05:38,417
Chemicals and acid drainage
from old mines leak

82
00:05:38,457 --> 00:05:42,131
into rivers and groundwater
— the hydrosphere.

83
00:05:42,171 --> 00:05:47,612
Pits, waste rock and mine dumps change
the land permanently — the lithosphere.

84
00:05:47,652 --> 00:05:53,295
Dust and gases from blasting, crushing
and smelting pollute the atmosphere.

85
00:05:53,335 --> 00:05:58,809
And habitats are destroyed,
threatening biodiversity in the biosphere.

86
00:05:58,849 --> 00:06:04,698
Follow one chain to the end: blasting
releases dust, the dust settles on leaves,

87
00:06:04,738 --> 00:06:11,051
it blocks sunlight, photosynthesis drops,
and a nearby farmer's crop yield falls.

88
00:06:12,050 --> 00:06:14,940
For people, mining is a mixed blessing.

89
00:06:14,980 --> 00:06:17,858
It creates jobs, builds roads and towns,

90
00:06:17,898 --> 00:06:21,565
and earns billions of
rands in foreign income.

91
00:06:21,605 --> 00:06:25,906
But mine work is dangerous,
and breathing in fine dust

92
00:06:25,946 --> 00:06:30,919
causes lung diseases such as
silicosis and pneumoconiosis.

93
00:06:30,959 --> 00:06:35,865
That's why, by law, mining companies
must rehabilitate the land when

94
00:06:35,905 --> 00:06:40,666
mining stops: make the site safe
and stable, remove pollutants and

95
00:06:40,706 --> 00:06:46,339
treat the water, reshape the land and
replace the topsoil stored at the start,

96
00:06:46,379 --> 00:06:51,021
replant indigenous plants,
and restore rivers and wetlands.

97
00:06:52,017 --> 00:06:53,377
Quick recap.

98
00:06:53,417 --> 00:06:55,947
An ore is worth the cost of mining.

99
00:06:55,987 --> 00:07:01,545
Shaft mining reaches deep ore; open
cast mining takes ore near the surface.

100
00:07:01,585 --> 00:07:06,631
Refining separates the metal physically,
then chemically by reduction.

101
00:07:06,671 --> 00:07:10,267
And mines must be
rehabilitated when they close.

102
00:07:10,617 --> 00:07:13,367
Your turn — pause if you need to.

103
00:07:13,407 --> 00:07:16,933
One: in lead oxide plus
carbon gives lead plus

104
00:07:16,973 --> 00:07:20,693
carbon monoxide,
which substance is reduced?

105
00:07:20,733 --> 00:07:26,441
Two: at five grams per tonne, how many
tonnes of ore give one kilogram of gold?

106
00:07:26,481 --> 00:07:30,421
Three: which mining method
suits coal close to the surface?

107
00:07:35,471 --> 00:07:36,711
Answers!

108
00:07:36,751 --> 00:07:40,667
One: the lead oxide,
because it loses oxygen.

109
00:07:40,707 --> 00:07:42,677
Two: two hundred tonnes.

110
00:07:42,717 --> 00:07:45,017
Three: open cast mining.

111
00:07:45,057 --> 00:07:49,603
Nail those, and you're ready for
the mining questions in your exam.
